module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_application_binds_by_position_where_the_model_binds_by_name

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data an_application_binds_by_position_where_the_model_binds_by_name: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "an_application_binds_by_position_where_the_model_binds_by_name" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "TWO ROUTES ANSWER ONE QUESTION -- which argument feeds which parameter -- and they disagree about whether the children's SEQUENCE is part of the answer. The class is not a lowering bug: it is a fork of the binding AUTHORITY, and the model has already decided the question on the carrier that owns it, so the defect is that one route does not read what the model says.",
    "WHAT THE MODEL SAYS, structurally. A function's parameter set is the Arrow's DOMAIN, a `TypeNode { connective: Conj }` whose children are `Edge { label: Named { name } }` -- and `v2.std.node` `connective_edge_discipline` gives Conj `LabeledEdges`, which `canonicalize_labeled_for_content_hash` SORTS before hashing. So a parameter list's stored order is NOT identity-bearing: the parameter set is name-keyed, and the NAME is the binding key. The application is the other side: `behavior_edge_discipline` gives `Transform` `PositionalEdges`, which is not sorted, so the application carries an ORDER and, as lowered today, no names at all.",
    "WHERE THE FORK IS, cited by symbol. `v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold` `body_lower_named_arg_value_optional` decodes the `name ^dag_token_colon value` spine, matches the parameter binding as `_binding`, and returns ONLY the operand; `body_lower_call_arg_edge_optional` consumes that; `body_lower_transform_call` then builds the call as one operator edge plus one `Edge { label: Positional }` per argument. The name is READ and DROPPED at the one place it could have been carried. Downstream, `v2.compiler.infer` `infer_application_argument_inhabitance` recovers formals with `infer_formals_from_domain` (the Conj's STORED order) and arguments with `infer_transform_argument_targets` (`node_positional_child_targets`, stored order) and ZIPS them by index. So v2 binds declaration-order to call-site-order and consults no name. `v1.compiler.emit` `order_typed_call_args_from_semantics` does the opposite: it reads the `application_plan` that `v1.compiler.infer` built by parameter identity and renders arguments in FORMAL order, selecting each by `matched_argument_index`.",
    "THE SHARPEST STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT IS INTERNAL TO v2 AND DOES NOT NEED v1 AT ALL: the zip reads the Conj's stored order as if it were a fact, when the same module's content-hash canonicalizer sorts that order away. Two folds in one compiler disagree about whether a parameter list has a sequence. A node that round-tripped through canonicalization would rebind its own call sites differently.",
    "RUNG: NOT ESTABLISHED ON THE v2 PATH, AND THAT IS THE REPORTED RUNG (DESIGN section 4b(1): a class's rung is the MINIMUM across its in-scope paths, and it must equal what EXECUTED evidence establishes). v2 is in scope and is UNMEASURED -- the one attempt failed at its own positive control -- so this row may not carry a rung, and specifically may not borrow one from the v1 evidence that did execute. An earlier version of this row said `RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor` and supported it with a v1 citation; that is citing the strong path while the other stays silent, which is the inflation section 4b(1) exists to catch, and it was corrected here rather than defended. WHAT THE SOURCE DERIVATION PREDICTS, labelled as a prediction: below the floor, because `applications bind in exact bijection` is a floor sentence and a binding chosen by a sequence the model does not carry is not a bijection the source wrote; and the loss would be SILENT wherever the mis-bound arguments share a representation -- the v1 side names that hazard in its own enrolled evidence, `v1.compiler_tests_rust` `ct_direct_call_formal_authority_carrier_test`, which asserts `matched_argument_index` explicitly because `positional carriage is silently wrong and still typechecks` when the two formals are both String. That prediction becomes the reported rung when a v2 observation exists and not before.",
    "CEILING: structurally impossible, and the construction is nameable rather than aspirational -- an application whose arguments are keyed by parameter identity has no slot in which a sequence could be the answer. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: the lowered application carries the argument NAME on the carrier that owns the binding, and every consumer (inhabitance, evaluation, emission) reads the binding from that one place -- so that dropping the name, or answering from child order, has no constructor. A v2 patch that carries the name AND keeps positional order as a second answer retires nothing: it makes the corpus carry both authorities instead of one.",
    "THE EVIDENCE STANDING IS ASYMMETRIC AND MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. The v1 half is EXECUTED: `test.claim.named_argument_binding_two_route_witness_test` compiles two fixtures differing only in the order two named arguments are written, at DIFFERENT declared types, and v1 carries zero blocking diagnostics on both -- which is what binding by parameter identity looks like from outside. The v2 half is NOT executed and is a declared frontier (DESIGN section 3c). An earlier form of that witness compiled the same fixtures through `v2.compiler.compile` `compile_dag_source_to_target_text`; on the required floor the v2 POSITIVE CONTROL RETURNED FALSE, meaning v2 refused the IN-DECLARATION-ORDER call too. A witness whose control fails cannot separate `v2 binds by position` from `v2's standalone driver cannot compile this fixture at all`, so those arms established nothing about binding and were REMOVED rather than reinterpreted. The same run measured that arm at 220565 eval steps against a 72300 budget and 567ms against a 302ms enrolment margin, so an interpreted whole-corpus compile is not viable as a floor witness even once it reaches its subject. FRONTIER TRIGGER, AND THE COST TERM IS PART OF IT RATHER THAN A CAVEAT BESIDE IT: a v2 acceptance harness that admits peer roots for a fixture source AND reaches its verdict inside a declared cost ceiling, AND whose refusal is readable as a DIAGNOSTIC rather than as a Bool. All three are load-bearing. `make the arm run` is satisfied by something 3x over budget that then cannot enrol, so the trigger would retire while the evidence stayed unobtainable; and a Bool-returning witness over a compile is a channel that cannot carry the one thing this lane needed -- WHY v2 refused -- which is why the control failure above is still only a hypothesis.",
    "SO THE v2 SIDE OF THIS ROW IS A SOURCE DERIVATION AND CLAIMS NO EXECUTION, which is stated here rather than left for a reader to discover. The derivation is checkable in one file and needs no run -- the two folds in `v2.compiler.infer` above -- but it has not been confirmed by executing the compiler, and the one attempt to confirm it failed at its control. Anyone citing this row for a BEHAVIOURAL fact about v2 is citing something that has not been measured.",
    "A THIRD FAILURE MODE OF THE EVIDENCE ITSELF, kept because the shape is more tempting than it looks (review 70059). The v2 arm was first written as an assertion of the NEGATION -- that v2 REFUSES the reordered call -- and that was wrong three ways: it forked the admission mechanism the repository already owns (`gunbc.explicit_witness_admission` `known_red_probe` over `std.witness_admission` `ExpectAssertionFalse`), so the hole entered no roster; it inverted the alert signature, because the repair would have turned the arm RED and made a fix indistinguishable from a regression; and it did not discriminate, because the probe collapsed every `Rejected` to false and so stayed green on ANY refusal cause. That last one is exactly what the floor then demonstrated: v2 refuses the control too, so a negated arm would have been GREEN for a reason with nothing to do with binding, and the hole would have been recorded as confirmed. The corpus already carried the rule -- the admission rows at `dag/test/claim/algebra_receiver_callable_witness_test.dag` state that the assertion is written in the acceptance direction on purpose, because asserting the refusal encodes the defect as the expected state.",
    "THE PART THAT IS NOT THIS LANE'S TO DECLARE, stated so the trigger is not mistaken for a small edit. Making a named call's identity independent of its argument sequence is a change to `v2.std.node` `behavior_edge_discipline` -- Transform would need the hybrid `canonicalize_arrow_labeled` already has (the operator edge positional and first, the named argument edges sorted) -- and that is the node identity law, which changes the content hash of every application in the corpus. What a mixed named/positional call does must REFUSE rather than widen (DESIGN section 5): there is no third authority that could say `these three by name and this one by the second slot`.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
